NOTE_SECTIONS = {
    "arrays": """# Arrays\n\n## Core idea\nArrays provide contiguous storage and O(1) indexed access. Most interview problems are solved by preserving an invariant while scanning, sorting, or building auxiliary prefix information.\n\n## Patterns\n- Prefix sums turn repeated range totals into O(1) queries after O(n) preprocessing.\n- In-place partitioning keeps memory O(1) when a write pointer and read pointer are enough.\n- Frequency arrays are effective when the value domain is small and bounded.\n\n## Complexity\nIndex access is O(1), a full scan is O(n), comparison sorting is usually O(n log n), and nested scans are only safe when one pointer never moves backward.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Mixing inclusive and exclusive prefix boundaries.\n- Mutating an array while iterating over indices that are still needed.\n- Forgetting integer overflow in cumulative sums.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef prefix_sums(values: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    prefix = [0]\n    for value in values:\n        prefix.append(prefix[-1] + value)\n    return prefix\n```\n""",
    "strings": """# Strings\n\n## Core idea\nTreat a string as a sequence of symbols and choose a representation that makes the invariant visible: counts, two pointers, a stack, or dynamic programming over positions.\n\n## Patterns\n- Normalize case or punctuation only when the specification allows it.\n- Use a frequency map for anagrams and a window for substring constraints.\n- Palindrome checks compare mirrored positions and can run in O(n) time.\n\n## Complexity\nA single pass is O(n). Building a mutable character buffer avoids accidental O(n²) concatenation in languages with immutable strings.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Treating bytes and Unicode characters as interchangeable.\n- Resetting a window too aggressively instead of moving its left boundary.\n- Assuming a sorted character list preserves original positions.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef is_palindrome(text: str) -> bool:\n    left, right = 0, len(text) - 1\n    while left < right:\n        if text[left] != text[right]:\n            return False\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\n    return True\n```\n""",
    "hashing": """# Hashing\n\n## Core idea\nHash tables trade memory for average O(1) lookup. Store the smallest useful fact: a count, an index, a boolean, or a partial aggregate.\n\n## Patterns\n- Count frequencies before a second pass when a complement or duplicate is needed.\n- Store the earliest index for longest-subarray problems.\n- Use a set when only membership matters.\n\n## Complexity\nExpected insert, lookup, and delete are O(1); a complete pass is O(n) with O(n) auxiliary storage. Worst-case guarantees depend on the language implementation.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Overwriting the earliest index when the earliest occurrence is required.\n- Using a mutable object as a key without understanding its hash semantics.\n- Forgetting that keys and values may have different types.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef first_pair(values: list[int], target: int) -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n    seen: dict[int, int] = {}\n    for index, value in enumerate(values):\n        needed = target - value\n        if needed in seen:\n            return seen[needed], index\n        seen[value] = index\n    return None\n```\n""",
    "two-pointers": """# Two Pointers\n\n## Core idea\nTwo pointers are useful when the search space has an ordering or when removing a left element makes a monotonic change. Each pointer should move in one direction.\n\n## Patterns\n- Opposite ends for sorted pair sums and palindrome-like checks.\n- Same direction for compaction, partitioning, and merging.\n- Fast/slow pointers for cycle and midpoint problems.\n\n## Complexity\nIf both pointers only advance, the total time is O(n), not O(n²). The proof comes from charging each pointer movement once.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Moving the wrong pointer after a comparison.\n- Skipping duplicates inconsistently.\n- Applying the pattern to data without the monotonic property it needs.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef has_pair(values: list[int], target: int) -> bool:\n    left, right = 0, len(values) - 1\n    while left < right:\n        total = values[left] + values[right]\n        if total == target:\n            return True\n        if total < target:\n            left += 1\n        else:\n            right -= 1\n    return False\n```\n""",
    "sliding-window": """# Sliding Window\n\n## Core idea\nMaintain a contiguous window that is either valid or intentionally moving toward validity. Expand with the right pointer and shrink from the left when the constraint breaks.\n\n## Patterns\n- Fixed-size windows for rolling sums and averages.\n- Variable windows with a counter for at-most or at-least constraints.\n- Last-seen indices when the window must contain unique symbols.\n\n## Complexity\nEach element enters and leaves at most once, so a well-formed window is O(n) time and O(k) space for the maintained state.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Checking the answer before shrinking an invalid window.\n- Deleting the wrong count when a value leaves.\n- Confusing “at most k” with “exactly k”; often use the difference of two at-most counts.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef longest_at_most_k(values: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    counts: dict[int, int] = {}\n    left = best = 0\n    for right, value in enumerate(values):\n        counts[value] = counts.get(value, 0) + 1\n        while len(counts) > k:\n            counts[values[left]] -= 1\n            if counts[values[left]] == 0:\n                del counts[values[left]]\n            left += 1\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n    return best\n```\n""",
    "binary-search": """# Binary Search\n\n## Core idea\nBinary search is a proof about a monotonic predicate. Define what the search interval means, choose a midpoint safely, and eliminate half the interval every iteration.\n\n## Patterns\n- Exact lookup in a sorted array.\n- Lower/upper bound for the first valid position.\n- Binary search on the answer when feasibility is monotonic.\n\n## Complexity\nO(log n) predicate checks and O(1) extra memory for iterative search.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Using an inconsistent inclusive/exclusive interval.\n- Infinite loops caused by not discarding mid.\n- Forgetting to prove feasibility is monotonic before searching an answer.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef lower_bound(values: list[int], target: int) -> int:\n    left, right = 0, len(values)\n    while left < right:\n        middle = left + (right - left) // 2\n        if values[middle] < target:\n            left = middle + 1\n        else:\n            right = middle\n    return left\n```\n""",
    "linked-list": """# Linked Lists\n\n## Core idea\nA linked list problem is usually about pointer ownership. Draw the nodes, name the previous/current/next pointers, and rewire exactly one edge at a time.\n\n## Patterns\n- A sentinel node simplifies insertion and deletion at the head.\n- Fast and slow pointers find midpoints and cycles.\n- Reverse links by saving `next` before changing `current.next`.\n\n## Complexity\nTraversal is O(n) and pointer rewiring is O(1) per node. Random access is O(n), unlike arrays.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Losing the rest of the list before saving the next pointer.\n- Returning the old head after a reversal.\n- Dereferencing a pointer before checking for null.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef reverse(head):\n    previous = None\n    current = head\n    while current is not None:\n        following = current.next\n        current.next = previous\n        previous, current = current, following\n    return previous\n```\n""",
    "stack-queue": """# Stack & Queue\n\n## Core idea\nA stack exposes the newest item first; a queue exposes the oldest. Match the abstraction to the order in which constraints become known.\n\n## Patterns\n- Monotonic stacks find the next greater/smaller item.\n- BFS uses a queue and marks a node when it is enqueued.\n- A deque supports both ends and is useful for window maxima.\n\n## Complexity\nPush/pop/enqueue/dequeue are O(1) with the right container. A monotonic scan is O(n) because each item is pushed and popped once.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Marking graph nodes only when dequeued, causing duplicates.\n- Using a list operation that shifts every element for queue removal.\n- Forgetting to pop a stack item before using the new top.\n\n## Template\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef bfs(graph: dict[int, list[int]], start: int) -> list[int]:\n    queue = deque([start])\n    seen = {start}\n    order = []\n    while queue:\n        node = queue.popleft()\n        order.append(node)\n        for neighbor in graph.get(node, []):\n            if neighbor not in seen:\n                seen.add(neighbor)\n                queue.append(neighbor)\n    return order\n```\n""",
    "recursion-backtracking": """# Recursion & Backtracking\n\n## Core idea\nWrite the recursive contract before the code: what does the function return for the current state? Backtracking adds a choice, recurses, and undoes the choice.\n\n## Patterns\n- Choose/skip for subsets and combination sums.\n- Swap positions for permutations.\n- Row-by-row placement with pruning for constraint grids.\n\n## Complexity\nThe search tree is often exponential; pruning and constraint ordering determine whether it is practical. Extra space includes recursion depth and the current path.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Sharing mutable paths between result entries.\n- Forgetting to undo a set, board cell, or frequency count.\n- Pruning before checking a valid complete state.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef subsets(values: list[int]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    result: list[list[int]] = []\n    path: list[int] = []\n    def visit(index: int) -> None:\n        if index == len(values):\n            result.append(path.copy())\n            return\n        visit(index + 1)\n        path.append(values[index])\n        visit(index + 1)\n        path.pop()\n    visit(0)\n    return result\n```\n""",
    "trees": """# Binary Trees\n\n## Core idea\nA tree recursive function should state the information returned by a subtree. Choose preorder, inorder, or postorder based on when the parent needs child results.\n\n## Patterns\n- BFS by level for shortest depth and views.\n- Postorder for height, balance, and subtree aggregates.\n- Carry a bound or path state for root-to-leaf constraints.\n\n## Complexity\nMost traversals are O(n) time and O(h) recursion/stack space, where h is the tree height.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Treating a missing child as a normal node.\n- Measuring edges and nodes inconsistently for height.\n- Mutating a shared path without popping on every return.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef inorder(root):\n    result = []\n    stack = []\n    node = root\n    while stack or node:\n        while node:\n            stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n        node = stack.pop()\n        result.append(node.value)\n        node = node.right\n    return result\n```\n""",
    "bst": """# Binary Search Trees\n\n## Core idea\nEvery node partitions values into a left range and a right range. Inorder traversal is sorted, which makes validation and kth-element queries natural.\n\n## Patterns\n- Carry exclusive lower and upper bounds for validation.\n- Maintain a predecessor/successor while descending.\n- Delete by replacing a node with its inorder successor or predecessor.\n\n## Complexity\nOperations are O(h); balanced trees give O(log n), while a skewed tree degrades to O(n).\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Comparing only with the parent instead of the full valid range.\n- Mishandling duplicates without defining a policy.\n- Returning the wrong subtree after deletion.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef valid_bst(root, low=None, high=None) -> bool:\n    if root is None:\n        return True\n    if low is not None and root.value <= low:\n        return False\n    if high is not None and root.value >= high:\n        return False\n    return valid_bst(root.left, low, root.value) and valid_bst(root.right, root.value, high)\n```\n""",
    "heaps": """# Heaps & Priority Queues\n\n## Core idea\nA heap keeps only the best element at the root, not the whole collection sorted. Use it when repeated access to the current minimum or maximum matters.\n\n## Patterns\n- Keep a size-k min/max heap for top-k problems.\n- Push the next item from each sorted stream for k-way merge.\n- Store `(priority, tie_breaker, value)` when stable ordering is needed.\n\n## Complexity\nPeek is O(1), push/pop are O(log n), and heapifying an array is O(n).\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Keeping the wrong heap polarity for top-k.\n- Assuming all elements are sorted after a heap operation.\n- Comparing values with incompatible types in tuple priorities.\n\n## Template\n```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef top_k(values: list[int], k: int) -> list[int]:\n    heap: list[int] = []\n    for value in values:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, value)\n        if len(heap) > k:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)\n    return heap\n```\n""",
    "graphs": """# Graphs\n\n## Core idea\nChoose the representation and traversal that matches the question: adjacency lists for sparse graphs, BFS for unweighted shortest paths, DFS for reachability, and topological order for dependencies.\n\n## Patterns\n- Mark visited when scheduling a node.\n- Relax edges for weighted shortest paths.\n- Union-Find tracks connectivity under incremental undirected merges.\n\n## Complexity\nBFS/DFS are O(V + E). Dijkstra with a binary heap is O((V + E) log V).\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Forgetting disconnected components.\n- Using BFS for arbitrary positive weights.\n- Treating a directed edge as bidirectional.\n\n## Template\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef shortest_unweighted(graph: list[list[int]], start: int) -> list[int]:\n    distance = [-1] * len(graph)\n    distance[start] = 0\n    queue = deque([start])\n    while queue:\n        node = queue.popleft()\n        for neighbor in graph[node]:\n            if distance[neighbor] == -1:\n                distance[neighbor] = distance[node] + 1\n                queue.append(neighbor)\n    return distance\n```\n""",
    "dynamic-programming": """# Dynamic Programming\n\n## Core idea\nDP works when a problem has overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure. Define a state that contains exactly the information needed for the future.\n\n## Patterns\n- Start with recursion plus memoization before compressing to a table.\n- Identify transition direction and base cases explicitly.\n- Use rolling arrays when the current state depends only on a small previous slice.\n\n## Complexity\nTime is usually number of states times transitions; space is the number of retained states.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Choosing a state that omits a constraint.\n- Mixing “ways”, “minimum”, and “maximum” recurrence meanings.\n- Updating a 0/1 knapsack row in the wrong direction.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef climb_ways(steps: int) -> int:\n    if steps <= 1:\n        return 1\n    previous, current = 1, 1\n    for _ in range(2, steps + 1):\n        previous, current = current, previous + current\n    return current\n```\n""",
    "greedy": """# Greedy Algorithms\n\n## Core idea\nA greedy algorithm commits to the best-looking local choice. It is correct only when an exchange or cut argument shows that an optimal solution can include that choice.\n\n## Patterns\n- Sort by finish time for maximum compatible intervals.\n- Sort edges by weight for Kruskal's minimum spanning tree.\n- Track the farthest reachable point for jump-style reachability.\n\n## Complexity\nMost solutions are dominated by sorting at O(n log n), followed by one linear pass.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Applying a local rule without proving it.\n- Sorting by the wrong endpoint or ratio.\n- Confusing feasibility with optimality.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef max_non_overlapping(intervals: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> int:\n    count = 0\n    end = float("-inf")\n    for start, finish in sorted(intervals, key=lambda pair: pair[1]):\n        if start >= end:\n            count += 1\n            end = finish\n    return count\n```\n""",
    "tries": """# Tries\n\n## Core idea\nA trie stores one node per prefix. It uses O(total characters) memory to make prefix operations independent of the number of stored words.\n\n## Patterns\n- Mark terminal nodes for exact-word queries.\n- Store counts for prefix frequency and autocomplete ranking.\n- Use a binary trie for maximum XOR queries.\n\n## Complexity\nInsert, lookup, and prefix search are O(L), where L is the key length.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Returning true for a prefix that is not a complete word.\n- Assuming a fixed alphabet when inputs may contain arbitrary symbols.\n- Forgetting to prune or preserve nodes during deletion.\n\n## Template\n```python\nclass TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.children = {}\n        self.terminal = False\n\nclass Trie:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = TrieNode()\n\n    def insert(self, word: str) -> None:\n        node = self.root\n        for char in word:\n            node = node.children.setdefault(char, TrieNode())\n        node.terminal = True\n```\n""",
    "bit-manipulation": """# Bit Manipulation\n\n## Core idea\nBits encode sets, parity, and compact state. Learn the identities first: XOR cancels equal values, `x & (x - 1)` clears the lowest set bit, and shifts multiply/divide by powers of two for non-negative integers.\n\n## Patterns\n- XOR for a unique element among pairs.\n- Masks to select or toggle subsets.\n- Bit counts for Hamming distance and subset enumeration.\n\n## Complexity\nMost fixed-width integer operations are O(1); iterating set bits is O(number of set bits).\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Confusing arithmetic and logical right shifts.\n- Ignoring signed integer width in languages with fixed-size integers.\n- Using `^` when exponentiation was intended.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef count_bits(value: int) -> int:\n    count = 0\n    while value:\n        value &= value - 1\n        count += 1\n    return count\n```\n""",
    "math": """# Math & Number Theory\n\n## Core idea\nTranslate the story into arithmetic invariants. Reduce large values with modular arithmetic, use GCD for divisibility structure, and precompute repeated prime facts.\n\n## Patterns\n- Euclid's algorithm for GCD.\n- Sieve of Eratosthenes for all primes up to N.\n- Fast exponentiation for logarithmic powers.\n\n## Complexity\nEuclid's algorithm is O(log min(a, b)); binary exponentiation is O(log exponent).\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Taking a negative remainder without normalizing it.\n- Overflowing before applying the modulus.\n- Treating floating-point geometry comparisons as exact.\n\n## Template\n```python\ndef gcd(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    while b:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    return abs(a)\n```\n""",
}


def notes_for_topic(slug: str, name: str) -> str:
    return NOTE_SECTIONS.get(slug, f"# {name}\n\nBuild a small invariant, test it on edge cases, and state time and space complexity before coding.\n")
